  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082348/assets/b77e20a705956f80379158fd88de70f95c94989c6bd7640d1f3c20d8cca7b4f5.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
